      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine patients' views on how clinical and demographic factors should affect priorities for cardiac revascularisation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A descriptive survey of patients' views conducted immediately after angiography and treatment counselling."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "136 patients who were awaiting coronary angioplasty in either of the two regional cardiology centres in Northern Ireland."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "About half the subjects (52%) felt that certain social factors such as having dependent relatives should be taken into account when deciding priority for surgery. A sizeable minority felt that younger subjects and non-smokers (40% and 44%, respectively) should be accorded higher priority, with older subjects and smokers being more likely to hold such views."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "While there is little evidence that demographic and lifestyle factors affect the relative efficacy of surgery, the challenge remains to devise a prioritisation guideline that can properly reflect societal values and the evidence base."
        }
      ]},
